= Templates and categories =

Templates and categories solve different problems.

* '''Template''' = reusable/transcluded content, structure or interface.
* '''Category''' = human-readable grouping and automatic index.
* '''Property''' = Semantic MediaWiki attribute or relationship.
* '''Book Matter''' = reusable content anatomy.
* '''Special page / validation category''' = generated operational state.

Do not use one merely because another would require more thought.

== What V1 actually implemented ==
The exhaustive public V1 archive contains:
* 9 Template namespace pages;
* 162 used category names;
* 0 authored Category namespace pages;
* 59 referenced-but-uncreated templates;
* 8 uncategorized templates;
* 1 unused template.

This differs materially from the larger system described in `Template Catalog`, `Template List`, `Template Usage Guide` and `CATEGORY ARCHITECTURE`. Those pages preserve valuable design intent, but intended components are not described as implemented until a corresponding live object exists.

== V1 template dispositions ==

=== Schema ancestor ===
<code>Template:Infobox</code> is transcluded by 80 pages. It is the main implemented structural template and must be treated as compatibility-critical during migration.

The live template supports only a small parameter set (title, type, status, related implementation and category), while later pages pass richer metadata. V2 must recover that richer schema from the source writings deliberately rather than assuming the template already enforced it.

=== Presentation family ===
The following live templates are primarily visual/navigation components:
* <code>Template:BITwiki complex decorative circle</code>
* <code>Template:BITwiki constructs decorative circle</code>
* <code>Template:BITwiki footer</code>
* <code>Template:BITwiki header element</code>
* <code>Template:BITwiki implementations decorative circle</code>
* <code>Template:BITwiki projects decorative circle</code>
* <code>Template:BITwiki simple decorative circle</code>

Their source and callers are preserved. They may be retired only after their remaining callers have been migrated to V2 navigation/presentation.

=== Unused historical shell ===
<code>Template:BITwiki</code> is the only unused live template. It is a large decorative Digital Codex header shell. Preserve it in the archive; do not treat it as semantic infrastructure.

=== Wanted templates ===
V1 has 59 wanted template titles. `Template:Documentation` alone is referenced 82 times; others represent proposed document headers, epistemic markers, evidence tiers, infobox variants, portals, citations and validation helpers.

A wanted template is a '''reference to a missing implementation'''. Preserve it as design-intent evidence. Do not bulk-create all wanted templates in V2.

== Canonical V2 template rules ==
A V2 template should have:
# a clear reusable purpose;
# documented parameters;
# one minimal usage example;
# explicit dependencies where relevant;
# source/provenance notes when inherited from V1;
# template documentation/categories inside <code>&lt;noinclude&gt;</code>;
# transcluding behavior inside <code>&lt;includeonly&gt;</code> only when that behavior belongs on caller pages.

Use templates as pattern languages, preserving the strong V1 principle: '''consistency without rigidity'''. A template should make repeated structure easier to understand and maintain; it should not force unrelated knowledge into one shape.

== V2 category rules ==
A created V2 Category page should state, when useful:
* what it groups;
* inclusion/exclusion criteria;
* parent category/categories;
* whether it is a domain, navigation group, project/meta grouping or generated operational category.

Category pages are indexes, not directories. A page may belong to multiple categories. Category hierarchy is a navigation graph, not the ontology.

See [[BITwiki:V1 category migration]] for the complete functional refactor of the 162 V1 category memberships.

== Maintenance surfaces ==
These are generated reports, not ordinary content pages:
* [[Special:AllPages]] — enumerate pages by namespace;
* [[Special:Categories]] — used categories;
* [[Special:UncategorizedPages]] — ordinary pages without category tags;
* [[Special:UncategorizedTemplates]] — templates without category tags;
* [[Special:UnusedTemplates]] — templates with no current transclusion caller;
* [[Special:WantedTemplates]] — referenced templates that do not exist;
* [[Special:WantedCategories]] — used categories without an authored Category page;
* [[Special:UnusedCategories]] — created category pages with no members;
* [[Special:BrokenRedirects]] / [[Special:DoubleRedirects]] — redirect hygiene.

== V1 → V2 migration ==
<pre>
archive exact source + complete revision history
→ capture transclusion/category membership
→ distinguish implemented object from design-only reference
→ compare variants
→ preserve unique writing and behavior
→ identify the function
→ choose correct V2 primitive
→ KEEP / REWRITE / MERGE / REDIRECT / RETIRE
→ implement
→ rerun Special-page validation
</pre>

== Release checks ==
Before a V2 deployment is considered clean:
# compare every live Template page against <code>archive-v1/templates/index.json</code>;
# do not delete `Template:Infobox` while legacy callers remain;
# review all 59 V1 wanted templates as design-intent evidence, not an automatic implementation backlog;
# review [[Special:UncategorizedTemplates]], [[Special:UnusedTemplates]] and [[Special:WantedTemplates]];
# review all category memberships against <code>archive-v1/categories/index.json</code>;
# review [[Special:Categories]], [[Special:WantedCategories]] and [[Special:UncategorizedPages]];
# verify semantic properties and category membership after template migrations;
# verify redirects/transclusions after every rename;
# do not deploy <code>archive-v1/</code> as live V2 content.

